,null,189,null,190,null,191,null,192,null,193,null,194,null,195,null,196,null,197,null,198,null,199,null,200,null,201,null,202,null,203,null,204,null,205,null,206,null,207,null,208,null,209,null,210,null,211,null,212,null,213,null,214,null,215,null,216,null,217,null,218,null,219,null,220,null,221,null,222,null,223,null,224,null,225,null,226,null,227,null,228,null,229,null,230,null,231,null,232,null,233,null,234,null,235,null,236,null,237,null,238,null,239,null,240,null,241,null,242,null,243,null,244,null,245,null,246,null,247,null,248,null,249,null,250,null,251,null,252,null,253,null,254,null,255,null,256,null,257,null,258,null,259,null,260,null,261,null,262,null,263,null,264,null,265,null,266,null,267,null,268,null,269,null,270,null,271,null,272,null,273,null,274,null,275,null,276,null,277,null,278,null,279,null,280,null,281,null,282,null,283,null,284,null,285,null,286,null,287,null,288,null,289,null,290,null,291,null,292,null,293,null,294,null,295,null,296,null,297,null,298,null,299,true]}],"solution":"class MyQueue:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.inbox = []    # newest on top\n        self.outbox = []   # oldest on top\n\n    def push(self, x: int) -> None:\n        self.inbox.append(x)\n\n    def _shift(self) -> None:\n        if not self.outbox:\n            while self.inbox:\n                self.outbox.append(self.inbox.pop())\n\n    def pop(self) -> int:\n        self._shift()\n        return self.outbox.pop()\n\n    def peek(self) -> int:\n        self._shift()\n        return self.outbox[-1]\n\n    def empty(self) -> bool:\n        return not self.inbox and not self.outbox\n","approach":"**Key insight: two stacks, with a lazy transfer.** Pushes go onto `inbox`. When a pop or peek needs the front and `outbox` is empty, pour all of `inbox` into `outbox`; the reversal puts the oldest element on top. Crucially, do *not* pour while `outbox` still has elements, because those are older than anything in `inbox`.\n\nWhy amortised O(1): every element is pushed onto `inbox` once, moved to `outbox` once and popped once, so n operations cost O(n) total even though a single pop may move many elements. This is the standard example interviewers use to check that you can explain amortised analysis.\n\nThe naive alternative makes `push` O(n) by always moving everything so the front is on top; it is correct but every push pays the full cost.\n\n**Common mistakes:** transferring on every pop (destroys the order and the complexity); `empty` checking only one stack; using Python list operations such as `pop(0)` or `insert(0, x)`, which are not stack operations and defeat the exercise.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Implement a stack with queues (the mirror problem, where push or pop has to rotate the queue). Thread safety: a lock around the transfer is required because it mutates both stacks; a two-lock queue (Michael-Scott style) is the classic concurrent design. Bounded capacity: track the total size and block or reject on push.","complexity":{"time":"O(1) amortised per operation","space":"O(n)"},"similar":["implement-stack-using-queues","min-stack","design-browser-history"],"number":173}